.TH PHIST 1 phist\-VERSION
.SH NAME
phist \- phi's simple terminal (st fork)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B phist
.RB [ \-aiv ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR class ]
.RB [ \-d
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR font ]
.RB [ \-g
.IR geometry ]
.RB [ \-n
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR iofile ]
.RB [ \-T
.IR title ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR title ]
.RB [ \-l
.IR line ]
.RB [ \-w
.IR windowid ]
.RB [[ \-e ]
.IR command
.RI [ arguments ...]]
.PP
.B phist
.RB [ \-aiv ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR class ]
.RB [ \-d
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR font ]
.RB [ \-g
.IR geometry ]
.RB [ \-n
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR iofile ]
.RB [ \-T
.IR title ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR title ]
.RB [ \-w
.IR windowid ]
.RB \-l
.IR line
.RI [ stty_args ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B phist
is an X11 terminal emulator forked from st.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-a
disable alternate screens in terminal
.TP
.BI \-c " class"
defines the window class (default phist).
.TP
.BI \-d " path"
sets the initial working directory to
.IR path .
Exits with an error if the directory cannot be entered.
.TP
.BI \-f " font"
defines the
.I font
to use when phist is run.
.TP
.BI \-g " geometry"
defines the X11 geometry string.
The form is [=][<cols>{xX}<rows>][{+-}<xoffset>{+-}<yoffset>]. See
.BR XParseGeometry (3)
for further details.
.TP
.B \-i
will fixate the position given with the -g option.
.TP
.BI \-n " name"
defines the window instance name (default phist).
.TP
.BI \-o " iofile"
writes terminal output to
.I iofile.
This feature is useful when recording phist sessions. A value of "-" means
standard output. Applications can only start, stop or trigger printing with
Media Copy sequences (CSI i) when this option is given.
.TP
.BI \-T " title"
defines the window title (default 'phist').
.TP
.BI \-t " title"
defines the window title (default 'phist').
.TP
.BI \-w " windowid"
embeds phist within the window identified by
.I windowid
.TP
.BI \-l " line"
use a tty
.I line
instead of a pseudo terminal.
.I line
should be a (pseudo-)serial device (e.g. /dev/ttyS0 on Linux for serial port
0).
When this flag is given
remaining arguments are used as flags for
.BR stty(1).
By default phist initializes the serial line to 8 bits, no parity, 1 stop bit
and a 38400 baud rate. The speed is set by appending it as last argument
(e.g. 'phist -l /dev/ttyS0 115200'). Arguments before the last one are
.BR stty(1)
flags. If you want to set odd parity on 115200 baud use for example 'phist -l
/dev/ttyS0 parenb parodd 115200'. Set the number of bits by using for
example 'phist -l /dev/ttyS0 cs7 115200'. See
.BR stty(1)
for more arguments and cases.
.TP
.B \-v
prints version information to stderr, then exits.
.TP
.BI \-e " command " [ " arguments " "... ]"
phist executes
.I command
instead of the shell.  If this is used it
.B must be the last option
on the command line, as in xterm / rxvt.
This option is only intended for compatibility,
and all the remaining arguments are used as a command
even without it.
.SH SHORTCUTS
.TP
.B Break
Send a break in the serial line.
Break key is obtained in PC keyboards
pressing at the same time control and pause.
.TP
.B Ctrl-Print Screen
Toggle if phist should print to the
.I iofile.
.TP
.B Shift-Print Screen
Print the full screen to the
.I iofile.
.TP
.B Print Screen
Print the selection to the
.I iofile.
.TP
.B Shift-Page Up
Scroll history up by one screen minus one line.
.TP
.B Shift-Page Down
Scroll history down by one screen minus one line.
.TP
.B Ctrl-/
Search scrollback and screen text as you type. Up/Down or Ctrl-P/Ctrl-N
move between matches, wrapping at either end, and Home/End go to the first
and last match. Page Up/Page Down go to the nearest match above or below
the rows in view, or on the first or last page, to the first or last
match. Enter keeps the result in
view; Escape or Ctrl-C restores the previous view. Backspace or Delete
removes the last character and Ctrl-U clears the query. Search is literal
and case-sensitive, follows soft wraps and includes the main screen beneath
alternate-screen programs.
The bar shows the current match's number and the total, such as 2/5.
.TP
.B Ctrl-Plus or Ctrl-Shift-Plus
Increase font size.
.TP
.B Ctrl-Minus
Decrease font size.
.TP
.B Ctrl-0
Reset to default font size.
.TP
.B Ctrl-Shift-y
Paste from primary selection (middle mouse button).
.TP
.B Ctrl-Shift-c
Copy the selected text to the clipboard selection.
.TP
.B Ctrl-Shift-v
Paste from the clipboard selection.
.SH WORKING DIRECTORY REPORTS
OSC 7 reports publish a file URI as the
.B _PHIST_CWD_URI
X11 window property of type UTF8_STRING. Read it with
.B xprop \-id \(dq$WINDOWID\(dq _PHIST_CWD_URI
or watch changes with
.B xprop \-spy \-id \(dq$WINDOWID\(dq _PHIST_CWD_URI\&.
The hostname and percent encoding are preserved. Reports are limited to
16,384 bytes; malformed reports are ignored. The property is absent until
a valid report and is removed by an empty report or terminal reset.
phist never changes directory in response to OSC 7. Consumers must check
the host before using the decoded path as a local directory.
.SH HYPERLINKS
Click an OSC 8 hyperlink label to reveal its URI in a fading bar below the
label, or above it when there is no room below.
Click the displayed URI or press Enter to copy it to PRIMARY and CLIPBOARD.
The target flashes for urlflashtimeout (150 ms by default), then the bar
closes. Disabling copy feedback closes it immediately.
Clicking elsewhere dismisses the bar. Escape dismisses it without sending
input. Other input keys dismiss it and run normally; modifier keys alone
leave it open.
Long targets scroll horizontally with the wheel, Up/Down or Page Up/Page Down.
Selecting a label copies its visible text. Automatically detected URLs
copy their targets directly when clicked.
.SH CUSTOMIZATION
.B phist
can be customized by creating a custom config.h and (re)compiling the source
code. This keeps it fast, secure and simple.
